`finishDrawing()` exists. But wait — is calling `finishDrawing()` without `draw()` correct? `finishDrawing()` in WorldRenderer resets state and un-enables the buffer. Let me look at it.

Actually, the simpler and cleaner fix: just skip the whole tessellator block when oldDebugHitbox is on. Let me reconsider my RenderManager edit — I wrote:

```java
Tessellator tessellator = Tessellator.getInstance();
WorldRenderer worldrenderer = tessellator.getWorldRenderer();
Vec3 vec3 = entityIn.getLook(partialTicks);
worldrenderer.begin(3, DefaultVertexFormats.POSITION_COLOR);

if (!OldAnimationsSettings.oldDebugHitbox)
{
    worldrenderer.pos(...)...endVertex();
    worldrenderer.pos(...)...endVertex();
    tessellator.draw();
}
else
{
    worldrenderer.finishDrawing();
}
```

Calling `begin()` then `finishDrawing()` without `draw()` — `finishDrawing()` calls `this.endAddingVertices()`? Let me check. Actually calling `begin()` and then `finishDrawing()` directly should be safe: `finishDrawing` calls `this.bufferOffset` reset + `disableVertexArray` etc. But the buffer was started by `begin()` which enables the color/position buffers and starts the draw mode. `finishDrawing()` typically ends adding and resets.

Hmm, but actually cleaner: don't call `begin()` at all when we're going to draw nothing. Let me restructure to just guard the entire block.

