These are all harness bugs, not code bugs:

1. My `clearMemory` zeroes ALL primitive statics including the *initializers*. So `blockHit = true` default is wiped to false. That's the harness destroying the class's default values, not a code defect. I need `clearMemory` to restore defaults instead of zeroing — the right way is to call the class's own default-reset, or to skip fields I don't intend to clear.

   Better approach: instead of reflection-zeroing, delete the config file and call the class's `reset()`-equivalent. But `reset()` also saves. Hmm.

   Cleanest: for "defaults" tests, don't clearMemory at all — the JVM starts with the real field initializers. Just delete the config file and call `load()`. For subsequent tests, set state explicitly via public API rather than zeroing.

   Let me restructure: 
   - Test 1 (defaults): no clearMemory (fresh JVM), delete file, load().
   - For later tests: explicitly set every field I depend on, or use a helper that applies the documented defaults via reflection by reading them once at startup.

   Best: capture the pristine defaults at class-init time (before any mutation) into a map, and restore from that map in clearMemory. That's robust and doesn't duplicate the defaults in the test.

2. `GL_INVALID_OPERATION` is `static final int` — my clearMemory skips FINAL only in the reference-null loop, but the primitive loop doesn't check FINAL. Fix: skip final in the primitive loop too.

Let me rewrite clearMemory properly.

Also note `MotionBlur.lastErrorCheck` etc. get zeroed which is fine.

Let me rewrite:

```java
static final Map<Class<?>, Map<Field, Object>> PRISTINE = new HashMap<>();

static void snapshot(Class<?> owner) throws Exception
{
    Map<Field, Object> m = new LinkedHashMap<>();
    for (Field f : owner.getDeclaredFields())
    {
        if (!Modifier.isStatic(f.getModifiers())) continue;
        f.setAccessible(true);
        m.put(f, f.get(null));
    }
    PRISTINE.put(owner, m);
}

static void restore(Class<?> owner) throws Exception
{
    for (Map.Entry<Field, Object> e : PRISTINE.get(owner).entrySet())
    {
        Field f = e.getKey();
        try { f.set(null, e.getValue()); } catch (IllegalAccessException ignored) {}
    }
}
```

Snapshot must happen before any mutation, and after class init. `Animations` static init happens on first access. So call `snapshot(Animations.class)` at the very start of main, before any load()/set. Same for MotionBlur.

Note: non-final static refs (like `LOGGER`) will be captured too — fine.

Let me rewrite the test file's clearMemory and add snapshot/restore at the start of main, replacing clearMemory calls with restore.

Let me do that.

