Now I have the full picture. FpsOverlay's applySetting has the `Presence` complication for colour/autoColour ordering. I'll expose a package-private `applySetting(String key, String value)` overload that uses a fresh `Presence` — but then the ordering fix wouldn't apply.

Better: refactor FpsOverlay so the `Presence` post-step lives in `load()`, and `applySetting(key, value)` is package-private and takes an *optional* Presence. Simplest: add a package-private method:

```java
static void applySetting(String key, String value)
{
    applySetting(key, value, new Presence());
}
```

For the single-key path this is correct (each key applied individually). The Presence fixup only matters when reading a whole file, and `ClientConfig` writing means both keys are always written together in a fixed order (autoColour before colour, or colour before autoColour?).

Let me check what order save() writes: `autoColour` then `colour`. And the bug comment says: "`autoColour=true` and a leftover `colour=#...` would end up pinned to that stale colour - which is exactly what save() writes after /fps color auto." So after `/fps color auto`, save writes `autoColour=true` AND `colour=#FFFFFF` (the reset colour). Reading that back naively: autoColour=true then colour=#FFFFFF → autoColour=false. Bug! Hence the fixup: if colour present but autoColour absent → keep autoColour. But here BOTH are present...

Wait, re-read: `if (seen.colour && !seen.autoColour) { autoColour = false; }`. So if autoColour key is ABSENT but colour present → autoColour=false. That's the opposite. Hmm.

So the scenario: `autoColour=true` and `colour=#FFFFFF` both present. Naive read: autoColour=true, then colour=#FFFFFF sets colour but doesn't change autoColour (the colour branch only sets `colour`, not `autoColour`). So autoColour stays true. Correct already!

Let me re-read the colour branch: it sets `colour = parsed` only. So autoColour is untouched. So reading both keys gives autoColour=true, colour=#FFFFFF. Fine.

Then when is `seen.colour && !seen.autoColour` true? When the file has `colour=#...` but no `autoColour` line — e.g. a hand-edited file, or an older format. In that case, the intent is "the user picked a colour", so autoColour should become false. Makes sense.

So the Presence fixup handles files lacking the autoColour key. If ClientConfig always writes both keys, the fixup never triggers. Good — the single-key `applySetting(key, value)` overload is correct for GUI/command use.

But for ClientConfig *reading* client.cfg, I want the same fixup semantics for the fps section. Since ClientConfig will always write both keys, and a hand-edited client.cfg could omit one... To be faithful, I'll give ClientConfig's fps section the same treatment: track presence per section.

Simplest robust approach: ClientConfig reads the whole file into `Map<section, Map<key,value>>` first, then applies section by section, so it can implement the presence fixup for fps. That's cleaner anyway — lets me implement "reset missing keys to defaults" semantics per section too.

