All available. Now let me write the GUI.

Design:

```java
public class GuiClientSettings extends GuiScreen
{
    private static final int ID_DONE = 0;
    private static final int ID_RESET = 1;
    private static final int ROW_HEIGHT = 24;
    private static final int CONTROL_WIDTH = 200;

    private final SettingsList list;
    private final List<Row> rows;
    private GuiButton pressed;   // control currently under the mouse

    // Row: either a header or a setting with a control
}
```

Rows: build from ClientConfig.settings() grouped by section.
```java
private static final class Row {
    final String section;
    final ClientConfig.Setting setting;   // null for a header
    GuiButton control;                    // the button/slider, positioned during draw
}
```

For headers I need a `control == null`.

Layout: list width 220 (GuiSlot default). Control width 200, x = listLeft + (220-200)/2 = listLeft + 10. Hmm, `drawSlot(entryID, x, y, l, mouseX, mouseY)` gives `x = left + width/2 - getListWidth()/2 + 2`. So control x = x + 10, width 200. Good.

Row y: `y + 2`, height 20 (button height). ROW_HEIGHT 24 gives 4px gap. Good.

Button width: `x` passed is `listLeft + 2`; control at `x + 10` = `listLeft + 12`, spanning to `listLeft + 212`. List right edge = `listLeft + 220`. OK.

Hmm, but the scrollbar is at `width/2 + 124` = listLeft + 110 + 124 = listLeft + 234. So the control (ends at listLeft+212) doesn't overlap the scrollbar (starts listLeft+234). 

Now, `getListWidth()` = 220, `left = 0`, `width` = screen width. listLeft = 0 + width/2 - 110.

Now the screen:
- title at y=15
- list top = 32, bottom = height - 52
- Done button at height-40, Reset All at height-40 (side by side: Done center-left, Reset center-right). Or Done center, Reset to the left.
- filename hint at bottom

Let me use:
- Done: `width/2 + 4, height - 40, 200, 20`
- Reset All: `width/2 - 204, height - 40, 200, 20`
- hint text centered at `height - 16`

`initGui()`:
```java
this.buttonList.clear();
this.buttonList.add(new GuiButton(ID_DONE, this.width/2 + 4, this.height - 40, 200, 20, I18n.format("gui.done")));
this.buttonList.add(new GuiButton(ID_RESET, this.width/2 - 204, this.height - 40, 200, 20, "Reset All"));
this.list.setDimensions(this.width, this.height, 32, this.height - 52);
```
Wait — list is created in the constructor since it needs `mc`. `setDimensions` in initGui is right (called on resize).

But `GuiSlot.setDimensions(widthIn, heightIn, topIn, bottomIn)` sets left=0, right=widthIn. Good.

`rows` built in the constructor via `ClientConfig.settings()`. But at construction time (from the keybind / command) the game is in-game so settings exist. Fine. Actually, safer to build rows in `initGui()` so the list is fresh each time. Yes — build in initGui.

Now, sliders need a `GuiPageButtonList.GuiResponder`. Let me check its methods: `onTick(int id, float value)`, `updateClickableByCode(int)`, etc. Let me confirm.

Let me grep GuiResponder interface.

Then: the slider's `FormatHelper.getText(id, name, value)` returns the display string. But GuiSlider's ctor does `this.displayString = this.getDisplayString();` which calls the helper → fine, formatHelper is set before.

But `I18n.format(this.name)` is passed as `name` to the helper. My labels aren't keys → I18n returns the key. Fine.

Actually, since I set `displayString` myself every frame in `drawSlot`, the helper barely matters. But `mousePressed` calls `getDisplayString()` too. Let me supply a FormatHelper that returns `name + ": " + formatted` — using the helper means the slider's own display is always correct even between my draws. Simpler: pass `null` and let the default do `I18n.format(name) + ": " + func_175220_c()` (raw float). Then I overwrite in drawSlot with a nicer format. Both fine. I'll pass a FormatHelper so it's consistently nice, and also overwrite in drawSlot (to reflect external changes).

Careful: for integral settings the raw float shows "255.0". My FormatHelper should format. Let me have the slider hold a reference to its `Setting` and format accordingly.

Now the responder: `onTick(id, value)` → `setting.set(String.valueOf(value))`. But that writes the file on every drag pixel. Need to defer.

Add to ClientConfig:
```java
private static int deferredWrites;   // or a boolean dirty
public static void deferSaves() { deferring = true; }
public static void flush() { if (deferring) { deferring = false; saveInternal(); } }
```
and `save()` → `if (deferring) { dirty = true; return; }`.

So:
```java
private static boolean deferring;
private static boolean pending;

public static void beginBulkChange() { deferring = true; }
public static void endBulkChange() { if (deferring) { deferring = false; if (pending) { pending = false; save(); } } }
public static void save() { if (loading) return; if (deferring) { pending = true; return; } write(); }
```
`loading` also becomes a deferral. Let me unify: `loading` → set `deferring=true`, call `endBulkChange()` at the end. But `endBulkChange` only writes if pending; load() wants an unconditional write. So keep them separate:

```java
public static void save() {
    if (deferring) { pending = true; return; }
    write();
}
```
load(): `deferring = true; ...; deferring = false; pending = false; write();`

Good.

GUI usage:
- `mousePressed` on a slider → `ClientConfig.beginBulkChange()` then `slider.mousePressed(...)`. Hmm, order matters: `mousePressed` calls `onTick` which sets the value → save deferred. So begin bulk before calling mousePressed.
- `mouseReleased` → `endBulkChange()`.
- Also on `onGuiClosed()` → `endBulkChange()` to be safe (screen closed mid-drag, e.g. Escape). But Escape opens nothing... `keyTyped` Escape → `this.mc.displayGuiScreen(null)`. So yes, flush on close.

Now: `drawSlot` sets `control.enabled = !setting.isGatedOff() && control.visible`. And `visible = true` always. Greyed buttons show as disabled. Good.

Careful: GuiButton.mousePressed returns false when `!this.visible || !this.enabled`. Good, gated rows are unclickable.

Clicking a TOGGLE/CHOICE button: cycle.
```java
private void cycle(Row row) {
    ClientConfig.Setting s = row.setting;
    if (s.getKind() == Kind.TOGGLE) {
        s.set(Boolean.parseBoolean(s.get()) ? "false" : "true");
    } else {
        String[] choices = s.getChoices();
        int next = s.choiceIndex() + 1;
        if (next >= choices.length) next = 0;
        s.set(choices[next]);
    }
}
```
For CHOICE where current value isn't in choices (e.g. fps colour "#123456" not a preset), `choiceIndex()` returns -1 → next = 0 → first preset. Acceptable. But better: show the actual value and when clicked jump to the next preset after it, or the first. Let me make `display()` for CHOICE return the raw value, and cycling from -1 → 0 (first preset). Fine.

Hmm, one nuance: for the fps colour row when autoColour is ON, the row is gated off, so the user must turn off colour-by-FPS first. That's a bit awkward but predictable. Alternatively don't gate the colour row and instead have choosing a colour turn autoColour off (like `/fps color`). That's actually friendlier. Let me reconsider…

Predictability vs convenience. Vanilla-style: the colour row being greyed when auto-colouring is on clearly communicates "this has no effect". I'll keep the gate. And the tooltip/hint isn't available, but greyed-out is the standard signal.

Now sound on click: `control.playPressSound(this.mc.getSoundHandler())` for buttons. For sliders, vanilla doesn't play sound. Skip.

Header rows: draw centered section title in a dimmer colour (0xA0A0A0) plus maybe a subtle line. Also add an indent/small separation. Let me draw the header text left-aligned with a leading "» "? Keep simple: centered bold-ish via drawCenteredString with colour 0xFFFF55 (gold) — actually vanilla resource packs use 0xEEEEEE. Let me use 0xFFFFA0 for headers and 0xFFFFFF for buttons. Hmm, contrast: gold on dark grey reads well. Let me use 0xE0E0E0 for headers and let buttons be their normal texture. Actually to make sections obvious, gold headers are good.

Let me now write it.

Also: should I add a "Reset this section"? Skip — "Reset All" only, as planned.

One more: `onGuiClosed()` — GuiScreen.onGuiClosed() is public and empty. Override to flush + save.

Also `keyTyped` Escape → `this.mc.displayGuiScreen(null)`. Default GuiScreen.keyTyped handles Escape. Let me confirm: GuiScreen.keyTyped at line 80 handles Escape → `this.mc.displayGuiScreen(null)`. Yes, standard.

Now `drawScreen`:
```java
public void drawScreen(int mouseX, int mouseY, float partialTicks) {
    this.drawDefaultBackground();
    this.drawCenteredString(this.fontRendererObj, "Client Settings", this.width/2, 12, 0xFFFFFF);
    this.list.drawScreen(mouseX, mouseY, partialTicks);
    super.drawScreen(mouseX, mouseY, partialTicks);
    // filename hint
}
```
Careful: `super.drawScreen` draws buttonList. And `list.drawScreen` needs to happen... order: buttons at the bottom, list above; no overlap. Fine.

Wait: `GuiScreen.drawScreen` at line 67 — does it also handle `drawDefaultBackground`? Let me check lines 67-80. Typically:
```java
public void drawScreen(int mouseX, int mouseY, float partialTicks) {
    this.drawDefaultBackground();
