Two failures to investigate:

1. **"yaw is float limited"** — I asserted `build(player).contains("Yaw: 0.1000000000")` after setting `rotationYaw = 0.1F`. But `(double)0.1f` is `0.10000000149011612`, so it formats as `0.1000000015`. My assertion was wrong — the test expectation is bad, not the code. Let me fix the assertion to reflect reality: `0.1f` as double = 0.10000000149011612 → "0.1000000015". That actually *demonstrates* the float limitation nicely. Let me fix the expectation.

2. **"size is gated on the master"** — I asserted `!row("size").isGatedOff()` while enabled was FALSE. That was a logic error in my test: I checked gating BEFORE turning the master on. Looking at my code:

```java
check("size is gated on the master", !row("size").isGatedOff());
CoordsHud.setEnabled(true);
```

I set `row("enabled").set("false")` which turns the HUD off, then check `!isGatedOff()` — but it SHOULD be gated off when the master is off. My assertion name says "is gated on the master" but the assertion checks NOT gated. That's backwards. It should be `row("size").isGatedOff()` == true when master is off.

Let me fix: when master off → size should be gated off (true). Let me rewrite that block properly.

Let me fix both.

For #1, let me compute the exact expected: `(double) 0.1f` = 0.100000001490116119384765625. Formatted %.10f → "0.1000000015". Let me verify: 0.1000000014901161... → to 10 decimals: 0.1000000014|90116 → rounds to 0.1000000015. Yes.

Better: make the test compute it from the float itself rather than hardcoding, so it documents the relationship. Let me use a clearer assertion: check that the printed yaw equals `String.format("%.10f", (double) 0.1F)` — that's tautological. 

Instead, let me assert the meaningful property: a float yaw does NOT round-trip exactly, i.e., the printed value differs from "0.1000000000". That demonstrates the float limitation rather than asserting an exact literal that might be miscalculated.

Let me write:
```java
player.rotationYaw = 0.1F;
String yawLine = lineOf(CoordsHud.build(player), 1);  // second line
check("float yaw shows its own granularity, not a clean 0.1",
    !yawLine.contains("0.1000000000"));
eq("float yaw is printed from the widened float", yawLine.trim(),
    "Yaw: " + String.format(Locale.ROOT, "%.10f", (double) player.rotationYaw) + "  Pitch: -12.5000000000");
```

Hmm, that's partly tautological but the first part (not clean 0.1) is the meaningful assertion. Let me just do the "not clean" check plus assert the exact known string "Yaw: 0.1000000015". Let me verify 0.1f exactly.

0.1f = 0x3DCCCCCD = 0.1000000014901161193847656250
%.10f → 0.1000000015 (rounding the 11th digit: 0.1000000014|90116... → 10 decimals: digits after point: 1,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,1,4 | next digit 9 → rounds up → ...15)

So "0.1000000015". Let me use that, and also keep the "not clean 0.1" check which is the robust one.

Actually, let me make it robust AND explicit:
```java
player.rotationYaw = 0.1F;
String text = CoordsHud.build(player);
check("float yaw is not printed as a clean 0.1", !text.contains("Yaw: 0.1000000000"));
check("float yaw shows the widened value", text.contains("Yaw: 0.1000000015"));
```

That's precise and documents the point.

For #2, let me fix the gating assertions:
```java
row("enabled").set("false");
check("row setter turns the HUD off", !CoordsHud.isEnabled());
check("row setter leaves free look alone", !FreeLook.isEnabled());

// Sub-settings grey out when the master is off.
check("size greys out with the master", row("size").isGatedOff());
check("posX greys out with the master", row("posX").isGatedOff());
check("posY greys out with the master", row("posY").isGatedOff());

CoordsHud.setEnabled(true);
check("size is live while on", !row("size").isGatedOff());
...
```

Let me verify `isGatedOff()` semantics — from ConfigTest usage `gated(settings, section, key)` returns `isGatedOff()`, and the FPS sub-rows use `SECTION_FPS, "enabled"` as master. So yes, gated off when master off.

Let me make both fixes.

Also need to import Locale in the test if I use it — I already import java.util.Locale. Good.

Let me apply the fixes.

